write_timing_model runs one filtered arrival search per input pin and
transition, and each search scans every output pin: the cost is the
product of input and output pin counts times cone depth, not cell count.
On XiangShan's blocks that is 1 to 21 minutes per block (LsqWrapper,
7,600 pins, 1,270 s; Dispatch, 11,700 pins on 309 k cells, 639 s, three
times VecRegionModule's 1.3 M cells) and the tail of every hierarchical
iteration. This batches inputs: virtual arrival clocks on up to 64
inputs at a time, one propagation per batch.

parallaxsw/OpenSTA pull request 478 (MakeTimingModel: batch input
arrival propagations), its three commits rebased onto the OpenSTA
commit OpenROAD pins here (f89887b5). Carried to measure the batching on
34 real blocks; the upstream review found it slower on two small
designs, which is the question these numbers answer. Not upstreamed
from here. Retires on a //:bump onto an OpenROAD whose OpenSTA carries
the pull request, or is dropped if the measurement says so.

